WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.970
- Welcome everyone to your
coffee break with me here,

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:04.560
Heinrich on Firm Learning,

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.320
the channel where I want to
help you become successful

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.360
in the first years of your career.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.680
And one from you,

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.380
someone from the audience
sent me a slide deck

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:17.220
of one of the largest companies
in the world of Amazon.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:19.170
And it is a slide deck that was presented

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:21.990
in one of their quarterly conference calls

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.930
where they present the
latest financial figures

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:26.700
often to a set of analysts

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.770
and other important stakeholders

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:31.590
to give them an update of
how the company is going.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.810
The reason I want to
discuss this with you today

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.160
is because frankly,

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.090
this is one of the worst
slide sets of this importance

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.460
that I have ever seen created

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.110
by any of these large companies.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.000
I think this is quite remarkable.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.860
So I want to discuss with you first

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.990
what makes these slides so bad?

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:50.880
And second I want to
discuss what you could do

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.160
to improve them if at
any point in your career

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.360
you would be in the position

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.190
to create slides like this as well.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:57.990
So let's start by having a first look

00:00:57.990 --> 00:00:59.940
at the slides before
we go into the details.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.360
So here, the cover slide of
this already looks quite basic,

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.470
to say the least.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.660
The next one seems to be
some type of legal slide,

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.850
of course, very text heavy
but okay for a legal slide.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:09.683
I guess that's fine.

00:01:09.683 --> 00:01:11.670
And then these are the typical slides

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.890
that you see here in the deck.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:14.790
And I hope that you agree

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:16.890
without even going into the details here.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:20.370
Just the first look and
feel is just very bad.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.940
It looks like an absolute
amateur would have created this.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.640
Every intern who just
spent a couple of months

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.200
in professional services I would claim

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.710
would be able to create superior
slides compared to this.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:34.350
And now, before we look
into the slides on detail

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.790
let me first stress one point

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:38.550
which might actually be
the most important point.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:39.960
So what do you want to accomplish

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.060
in such a financial earnings call

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.500
where there are all these analysts,

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.750
all these investors to whom you talk to

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.300
and you share with them
the financial numbers.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:49.710
And this is exactly what's happened here.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.240
So just slide after slide,

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.070
they're just sharing some random numbers.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.410
What would an investor likely think

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:55.920
if the investor sees a slide like this?

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.020
So the free cash for
the available cash flow

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.560
seems to go significantly
down over the last year or so,

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.810
but there's no statement, no explanation,

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.460
no rational about this at all.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.250
The only comment is that
there's a long term goal

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.800
to optimize free cash flow.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.030
What does this mean?

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:15.840
How is this giving any
confidence to investors at all?

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.330
There's anyone at this company working

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:21.150
who has some type of plan
what needs to be done?

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.220
What was even driving this decline

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.690
in free cashflow for instance?

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:28.650
So all of these obvious questions
are not answered at all.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.470
This is more or less just a data dump.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:32.820
It lacks any narrative.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.350
It lacks any storyline.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.270
If you want to learn more
about what you can do

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.220
to actually improve this for your slides,

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.470
I created lots of videos
on this in the past.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:42.660
Probably the entry point
for this specific topic

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.210
would be the videos on
action titles that I created.

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:47.670
I will link it somewhere
above here for your reference,

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:50.190
but now let's go a bit
more into the detail.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:52.260
It's really the composition
of these slides.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.450
And let's just started
with the very first slide.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.580
And of course, many of the
things that I discuss here

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.430
on this slide are relevant to
all the other slides as well.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:03.000
And let's just first talk
about the aspect ratio of this.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.110
So this is a slide with
an aspect ratio of 4 to 3.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:10.530
And this is just so incredibly outdated.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:14.370
Even when I started in
consulting in McKinsey in 2013,

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:15.330
I believe it was,

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:18.600
almost no one was using
this 4 to 3 format anymore.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.330
Today, almost every slide that you create

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:24.030
are done in a 16 to 9 aspect ratio

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:25.350
where it gives you the benefit

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:27.450
that you have far more space on the slide.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:28.710
And it also gives you the benefit

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:31.410
that now there aren't
black bars to the left

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:32.243
and to the right.

00:03:32.243 --> 00:03:34.260
So this alone just
completely outdated, really.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.640
So also surprising they're doing it.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.140
Then second, let's look at this.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:39.330
One of the things that just
makes the slide look busy,

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:40.680
not really clean and nice,

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.500
is just all the different font sizes

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.360
that have been utilized here.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.160
So notice here how this long term goal,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:50.070
this is bigger that here,
the Q3 axis are smaller,

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:52.860
but then the axis on the
Y axis are bigger again,

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.410
then here, the numbers are
the same as on the Y axis.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:56.820
Also some other things, for instance,

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.730
these two dashes here, just
looked very unprofessional.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:00.660
Why would you do that?

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.700
What exactly is the meaning of this?

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.760
Now let's look also at the chart itself.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.560
So first of all there's one principle

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:08.910
that I want you to have in mind,

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.510
and this is the principle
of the data-to-ink ratio.

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:14.160
So just very basic principle

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:17.850
is that you always want to
show as much data as possible

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.340
with as little ink as possible.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:23.910
Ink referring to any lines that
you are drawing on the page.

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:26.820
Just a common thing that is
just not necessary at all.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:28.830
Especially if you have
bar charts like this.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.960
Just to have this additional Y axis.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:32.310
Why do you need this Y axis?

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:33.510
Just take it out

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:35.430
because then of course
you have the numbers

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:36.960
on top of the bars.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.420
This is at least what I
would recommend you to do

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:40.860
to have numbers on top of the bars

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:42.300
because for whatever reason,

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.100
these ladies and gentlemen
at Amazon decided

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.770
that they just include a
number on the very first bar

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:48.180
and the very last bar.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:49.350
Why is this the case?

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:53.220
So what if someone would want
to know what here in Q2, 2021,

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:55.260
the trading 12 months average was?

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:57.840
Is the person supposed to take out a ruler

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.600
and somewhat try to
understand based on the axis

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.670
what number here for Q2 is the right one?

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:05.100
In addition to this idea
of data-to-ink ratio,

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.100
why is there this weird
light blue background here

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:09.030
in this chart?

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:10.530
What value is this adding?

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:11.790
What is this saying?

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:13.080
This just wastes ink,

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:14.820
especially if you print out such a page.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.260
On a black and white printer,

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.600
depending on the contrast
setting of the printer,

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.000
you might not be even
able to just separate

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:22.860
the bar from the background anymore

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:25.080
because the background is colored as well.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.600
So just absolutely no need for doing that.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.570
Don't just randomly use
background colors like this

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:31.830
if it's not needed.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:35.070
Let's also just look at another
inconsistency of this page.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:36.690
So let's look at negative numbers.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:38.100
Let's first look here at the axis.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.640
So we see that this axis
goes down to -40,000,

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:42.750
actually here with this legend,

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:45.390
- 40 million US dollar, I guess,

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.520
but here now this is denoted as a million,

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:49.320
whereas here the negative number

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:51.630
is denoted with these two brackets

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:54.330
which is another way of
denoting a negative number,

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:55.470
which is a fine way.

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:58.020
You can do that, though I would
say not the most intuitive.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:05:59.430
I think not everyone understands this,

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:01.710
but if you do then at
least do it consistently

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:02.610
throughout the chart.

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:04.860
Why would you use the one in
notation and the one inside

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:07.020
and then the other annotation
at the other hand side?

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:08.220
Just another smaller thing

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:09.810
that I also talked about in some videos

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:11.910
is the use of this dollar sign.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:13.680
That's always a bit of a problem.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:15.210
Maybe you could argue the way for Amazon.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:16.530
It's clear that this is US dollar

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:18.030
but let's at least acknowledge

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:20.280
that there's so many dollars on the world.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:23.100
There are Canadian
dollars, Australian dollar,

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:26.160
Singapore dollar, Taiwanese
dollar and so on and so forth.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:27.750
And at least as far as I know,

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:29.820
all of them use this dollar sign.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:34.080
So don't use this dollar sign
to just denote the currency

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.630
but rather use the three
letter acronym, right?

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:39.930
So USD for US dollar, for instance,

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:42.150
if you would want to talk about dollars.

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:43.920
Next let's talk about alignment.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:45.450
So the basic idea is

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:48.300
when you arrange elements on such a page,

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:51.480
that there should be as
few just logical levels,

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:55.350
logical areas on the plane
of the slide as possible.

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:57.210
And just to start here at the left,

00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:59.700
usually you think about design spaces

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:02.340
where the slide starts at
a given point to the left

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:04.770
and ends at a given point to the right.

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:06.600
And this is maybe a minor comment,

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:07.433
but for sure,

00:07:07.433 --> 00:07:09.660
if you would work at a firm
like McKinsey, BCG, Bain,

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:11.850
I mean you will get feedback
on things like that, right?

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:13.710
So this bar here, the long term goals,

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:16.080
this border around it,
this starts to the left,

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.090
but actually the axis of the chart

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:20.400
starts further to the right of this.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:23.280
So it's not really aligned to
the left border of the page.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.860
And there's no apparent
hierarchical argument,

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:27.960
hierarchical reason why
that would be the case.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.050
Next let's look here at the
legend on top, the MM on top.

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.250
So this is neither center

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:34.560
to the $80,000 unit that is shown here

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.930
nor is it left aligned to this unit.

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:39.990
So just somewhere randomly
placed here on the chart.

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:42.408
Let's also look here to the
right side of this chart

00:07:42.408 --> 00:07:43.320
where you see that also here

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:46.620
the chart doesn't end nicely
aligned to the right border

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:49.470
of this border around the
long term goal text box.

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:51.060
Let's look here at the footnote

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:53.310
that also just seems to placed
here completely randomly.

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:55.380
It is neither aligned to the border here

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:56.590
of the long term goals box.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:59.340
It is also not aligned
to here, the dollar sign.

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:02.030
So it starts further to the
right than this dollar sign.

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:04.170
So just looks completely random.

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:05.310
And then last but not least,

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:08.160
let's also talk about
this turquoise box here

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:12.570
that says down 171% year on
year, I guess this means,

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:14.190
which is also just randomly
placed here, right?

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:15.600
So it's not nicely centered.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:17.610
You would expect this to be centered.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:18.443
It's also brief.

00:08:18.443 --> 00:08:20.250
You talk about the messaging of the page.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:21.690
We also briefly touched upon it

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:22.860
in the beginning of the video.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:23.820
So first of all,

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:25.063
I would always recommend to you,

00:08:25.063 --> 00:08:27.060
I think this is what the
professional standard is

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.060
to use the so-called
action titles on slides.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:31.980
So not just writing free cash flow

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:33.360
but using an action title,

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.400
but then even if we
read this takeaway box,

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:36.870
as you could call it here at the bottom.

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:39.930
It says Long-Term Goal
Optimize Free Cash Flows.

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:41.610
Again, what does this mean?

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:42.443
How do you know?

00:08:42.443 --> 00:08:44.820
So first of all, what
is the long term goal?

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.490
Second of all, how do they
optimize free cash flow?

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:48.323
If I look at this,

00:08:48.323 --> 00:08:50.370
they're not optimizing free
cash flow at all, right?

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:52.890
Free cash flow seems to
be going down like crazy.

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:54.810
So if they have a goal in
the future, what is the goal?

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:56.490
And what are the tangible measures

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:57.840
that they wanna put forward?

00:08:57.840 --> 00:08:59.520
How can any analyst trust

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:01.620
that they will succeed to
do that based on this slide?

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:03.240
I think that's a very big problem.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.340
Next, a slide like this,
where we talk about net sales.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:07.680
Again, just notice how
this is just a data dump.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:08.550
Just some numbers.

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:10.680
Again, the most important
numbers are even missing here

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:12.840
because it's not really
appearing on the bars.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.240
But let's all notice that this
turquoise bubble here on top,

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:17.520
so here it says up 7% year on year,

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:20.580
but then also up 9% F/X adjusted.

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:22.920
So F/X is the foreign exchange rates.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:24.420
But now if I look at the numbers,

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:26.580
are these now the adjusted numbers,

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.470
so the F/X adjusted numbers

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:31.188
or are these the non F/X adjusted numbers?

00:09:31.188 --> 00:09:32.190
And now there even a footnote.

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:33.150
Now I'm reading the footnote

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:35.100
but I'm not really getting
this from the footnote.

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:36.930
Of course, I cannot start doing the math,

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:39.960
start calculating whether 116 over 108

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.570
rather corresponds to 7% or to 9%.

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:43.440
To some I get to it,

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:45.840
but of course, this is
just very unprofessional.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:48.300
The numbers not labeled correctly.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:50.730
Just leave so many questions
when looking at this.

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:52.140
Now let's look at a slide like this.

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:52.973
So I think of course,

00:09:52.973 --> 00:09:54.060
many things I already commented on

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:55.350
are apparent here as well.

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:58.350
Of course here the use of
pie charts in the right

00:09:58.350 --> 00:09:59.580
is also problematic.

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:01.770
So I also created a video
about data visualization

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:03.570
in the past that I will
also link it above here,

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:06.000
